---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const BayModule = `-- bay.lua - a module is just a table you return

local bay = {}

bay.capacity = 6
local docked = 0

function bay.dock(count)
  docked = math.min(bay.capacity, docked + count)
  return docked
end

function bay.status()
  return docked .. " of " .. bay.capacity
end

return bay`;

const Figure1 = `local bay = require("bay")

print(bay.status())`;
const Figure1Out = `0 of 6`;

const Figure2 = `local ok, err = pcall(function()
  error("reactor offline", 0)
end)

print(ok, err)`;
const Figure2Out = `false\treactor offline`;

const Figure3 = `local function readCharge(source)
  assert(type(source) == "table", "expected a table")
  return source.charge or 0
end

print(readCharge({ charge = 98 }))
print(readCharge({}))`;
const Figure3Out = `98
0`;

const Task1 = `-- main.lua - Skynest bay controller

local bay = require("bay")

print("Capacity:", bay.capacity)

bay.dock(2)
print("Status:", bay.status())

bay.dock(9)
print("Status:", bay.status())

print("Hidden counter:", bay.docked)`;
const Task1Out = `Capacity:\t6
Status:\t2 of 6
Status:\t6 of 6
Hidden counter:\tnil`;

const Task2 = `-- main.lua - Skynest bay controller

local bay = require("bay")

print("Capacity:", bay.capacity)

bay.dock(2)
print("Status:", bay.status())

bay.dock(9)
print("Status:", bay.status())

print("Hidden counter:", bay.docked)

local function readCharge(source)
  assert(type(source) == "table", "expected a table")
  return source.charge or 0
end

print("Charge:", readCharge({ charge = 98 }))
print("Charge:", readCharge({}))`;
const Task2Out = `Capacity:\t6
Status:\t2 of 6
Status:\t6 of 6
Hidden counter:\tnil
Charge:\t98
Charge:\t0`;

const Task3 = `-- main.lua - Skynest bay controller

local bay = require("bay")

print("Capacity:", bay.capacity)

bay.dock(2)
print("Status:", bay.status())

bay.dock(9)
print("Status:", bay.status())

print("Hidden counter:", bay.docked)

local function readCharge(source)
  assert(type(source) == "table", "expected a table")
  return source.charge or 0
end

print("Charge:", readCharge({ charge = 98 }))
print("Charge:", readCharge({}))

local ok, err = pcall(readCharge, "not a table")
print("Survived:", ok, err)

local ok2, err2 = pcall(function()
  error("reactor offline", 0)
end)
print("Survived:", ok2, err2)`;

const Checkpoint = `Capacity:\t6
Status:\t2 of 6
Status:\t6 of 6
Hidden counter:\tnil
Charge:\t98
Charge:\t0
Survived:\tfalse\tmain.lua:16: expected a table
Survived:\tfalse\treactor offline`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/lua-programming/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 1.12: Modules and Handling Errors</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Concept -->
        <section id="concept">
          <h2>The Idea</h2>

          <p>
            One file is fine until it is four hundred lines. Lua's answer is
            small and, by now, predictable:{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">a module is a table that a file
            returns.</b> There is no module keyword and no export list.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={BayModule}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="bay.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p>
            Every idea in that file is one you already have. A table (1.10).
            Functions stored in it (1.7). A{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="local"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            captured by those functions, which makes it private (1.8). And the
            last line hands the table to whoever asked.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure1}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />
          <Code
            code={Figure1Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <CodeSnippet
              code={`require("bay")`}
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            finds <b>bay.lua</b> next to your file, runs it once, and gives you
            what it returned. <b>Note the name has no{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code=".lua"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            on it.</b>
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">"Runs it once" is the part worth
            remembering.</b> Require the same module from five files and the
            file executes a single time; everyone gets the same table, and so
            everyone shares its state. That is usually what you want and
            occasionally a surprise.
          </p>

          <p>
            The variable name on your side is yours to choose &mdash;{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="local dock = require('bay')"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            works just as well. The module has no say in what you call it, which
            is a nice consequence of it being an ordinary value.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">When something is actually wrong</h3>

          <p>
            Almost nothing in Lua stops your program. A missing variable is nil,
            a missing table key is nil, a wrong argument count is tolerated.
            You have been shown this repeatedly, and it is why{" "}
            <b>Lua bugs tend to surface far away from their cause</b>.
          </p>

          <p>
            <CodeSnippet
              code="error"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            is how you stop on purpose, and{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="pcall"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            &mdash; protected call &mdash; is how you run something that might
            fail without going down with it.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure2}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />
          <Code
            code={Figure2Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <CodeSnippet
              code="pcall"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            always returns at least two things: <b>whether it survived</b>, and
            then either the results or the error message. It is the multiple
            returns from Activity 1.7 doing something genuinely load-bearing.
          </p>

          <p>
            The{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="0"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            in{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code={`error("...", 0)`}
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            means <i>do not attach a file and line number</i>. Leave it out and
            you get{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="main.lua:2: reactor offline"
              lang={"txt"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />, which is usually more useful &mdash; you will see both forms in
            the checkpoint.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">Checking your assumptions</h3>

          <p>
            <CodeSnippet
              code="assert"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            is{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="error"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            with the condition built in: carry on if the value is truthy, stop
            with your message if it is not.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure3}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />
          <Code
            code={Figure3Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <b>Use it at the top of a function, on the arguments.</b> That is
            where an assert earns its keep &mdash; it moves the failure from
            wherever the bad value eventually caused trouble to the moment it
            arrived, which is the difference between a two-minute bug and an
            hour.
          </p>

          <p>
            Remember Activity 1.5 while you write them.{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="assert(count)"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            passes when count is <b>0</b>, because zero is truthy &mdash; that is
            the check you wanted. But it also passes for a string, a table, or
            anything else that is not nil, so assert what you actually mean:{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code={`assert(type(count) == "number")`}
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Do not reach for{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="pcall"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            everywhere.</b> Wrapping your whole program in one turns a crash with
            a line number into silence, which is worse. Use it where failure is
            expected and recoverable &mdash; reading a save file that might not
            exist, loading an image that might be missing &mdash; and let genuine
            bugs crash loudly while you can still see them.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            This activity needs <b>two files in the same folder</b>. Create{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">bay.lua</b> with the module shown above,
            then start a fresh <b class="text-sn-theme">main.lua</b> beside it.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Use your module</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Require the bay module and print its{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="capacity"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />.
            </li>
            <li>Dock 2 ships and report the status.</li>
            <li>
              Then dock <b>9</b> more and report again. The clamp inside the
              module should hold it at capacity.
            </li>
            <li>
              Finally, print{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="bay.docked"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              from outside. <b>It is nil</b> &mdash; the counter is a{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="local"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              inside the module, so nothing out here can reach it. That is a
              closure doing exactly what Activity 1.8 said it would.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code code={Task1} frame="code" lang="lua" title="main.lua" />

          <Code
            code={Task1Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Guard a function's input</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Write{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="readCharge(source)"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              that asserts its argument is a table, then returns{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="source.charge"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              or 0.
            </li>
            <li>Call it once with a charge and once with an empty table.</li>
            <li>
              Both should succeed. The assert is not doing anything visible yet,
              which is the normal state of a good assert.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task2}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            ins={{ range: "15-21" }}
          />

          <Code
            code={Task2Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Survive a failure</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Call{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="readCharge"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              with a <b>string</b> through{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="pcall"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />, and print both values it returns.
            </li>
            <li>
              Then raise your own error inside a{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="pcall"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />, this time with{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="0"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              as the second argument.
            </li>
            <li>
              Compare the two messages. <b>One carries a file and line number
              and one does not</b>, and that is the only difference the{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="0"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              made.
            </li>
            <li>
              The program should reach the end and exit normally. Nothing
              crashed, and you handled two failures.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task3}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            ins={{ range: "23-29" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Challenge (Optional)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Add an{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="undock(count)"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              function to the module that refuses to go below zero, and give it
              its own assert.
            </li>
            <li>
              Move the <b>Ship</b> class from Activity 1.11 into a{" "}
              <b class="text-sn-theme">ship.lua</b> module and require it. This
              is exactly how Unit 2 is organized, so it is worth doing once
              now.
            </li>
            <li>
              Require the bay module <i>twice</i> into two different variables,
              dock through one, and read the status through the other. Predict
              what you will see before running it &mdash; the answer follows
              from "runs it once."
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Check Your Work</h2>

          <p>Your finished file should produce this.</p>

          <Code
            code={Checkpoint}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <b class="text-sn-theme">That is Unit 1.</b> You have the whole
            language: five types, three loops, functions that return several
            things, closures, one data structure doing five jobs, and a way to
            split a program across files. Nothing has been held back for later.
          </p>

          <p>
            Unit 2 adds a window, a game loop, and a way to draw. It adds no new
            Lua. Every enemy will be an instance from Activity 1.11, every list
            of bullets will be walked backwards as in Activity 1.9, and every
            timer will be a closure from Activity 1.8.
          </p>
        </section>

        <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-5">
          <a
            class="btn btn-outline-light"
            href="/courses/lua-programming/unit-1/1-11"
          >
            <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Previous
          </a>
          <a
            class="btn btn-outline-primary"
            href="/courses/lua-programming/unit-2/2-1"
          >
            Next: Your First LÖVE Window <i class="si-arrow-right"></i>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
